 n sufficiently large) can be obtained from a tournament with some d vertices, where ε^{-1} ≪ d < c, by first replacing each vertex by a transitive set of size ⌊n/d⌋ or ⌈n/d⌉ (and directing edges within each transitive set according to some fixed ordering of V) and then reversing the direction of at most ½εn² edges. To see this we use the regularity lemma. Let T be an S-free tournament with vertex set V. Choose δ > 0 much smaller than ε, and take a δ-regular partition of V into say d sets all of size ⌊n/d⌋ or ⌈n/d⌉, where ε^{-1} ≪ d, and d is at most a constant depending on ε. Since the VC-dimension of the in-neighbourhood hypergraph of T is bounded (by 2.3), it follows that for each δ-regular pair (X, Y) of sets in this partition, either there are at most δ|X||Y| edges from X to Y, or at most δ|X||Y| from Y to X. Consequently T can be obtained from a d-vertex tournament as described earlier; and so the number of such T is at most 2^{εn²}. This proves 7.1.\n\n# 8  Acknowledgement\n\nThe authors would like to thank the Bellairs Research Institute of McGill University (Holetown, Barbados), for hosting a workshop at which much of this work was carried out.\n\n12--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChapter 6 Comparing Width Parameters 63 (https://pureadmin.qub.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/617157723/Graph_width_parameters_from_structure_to_algorithm.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; \\mathrm{mmw}(G) \\le \\mathrm{bw}(G) \\le \\mathrm{tw}(G) + 1 \\le 3\\mathrm{mmw}(G), \\qquad (6.3) ... • sim-width dominates tree-independence number, tree-independence number and sim-width are more powerful than mim-width, and twin-width is incomparable with these three parameters;\nChapter 6 Comparing Width Parameters 63\n\nthe independence number of \\(G[N_G(A_e)\\cap \\overline{A_e}]\\) is at most tree-\\(\\alpha(G)\\) and so \\(\\mathrm{cutsim}_G(A_e,\\overline{A_e}) \\le \\mathrm{tree}\\text{-}\\alpha(G)\\). 